Here's a C++ function that converts markdown formatted text to HTML formatted text:

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <string>
#include <sstream>

std::string markdownToHtml(const std::string& markdown) {
    // Convert the markdown to HTML
    std::stringstream htmlStream;
    htmlStream << markdown;
    return htmlStream.str();
}

int main() {
    std::string markdown = "# Hello, World!\n\nThis is a markdown text.\n\n- One\n- Two\n- Three";
    std::string html = markdownToHtml(markdown);
    std::cout << "Markdown: " << markdown << std::endl;
    std::cout << "HTML: " << html << std::endl;
    return 0;
}
```

This function uses a `std::stringstream` to convert the markdown text to HTML. The `std::stringstream` class allows us to write the markdown text to a stream, which automatically converts it to HTML. The `str()` method of the stream returns the HTML text as a string.

Note that this function assumes that the markdown text is well-formed and follows the markdown syntax. If the markdown text is not well-formed, the function may not work as expected. You may want to add additional error handling and validation to the function to handle invalid markdown text.

